Explanation of this in setTimeout
🔴 Why user.greetDelayed() Doesn't Work as Expected (Code 1 & 2):
In both original codes:
setTimeout(function() {
  console.log(`Hello, ${this.name}!`);
}, 1000);



The function inside setTimeout is a regular function, not bound to the user object. So this inside it refers to the global object (window in browsers), not to user.

Thus, this.name becomes undefined, and you see:
Hello, undefined!




✅ Storing this in a Variable (e.g., self or that):

const user = {
  name: "Alice",
  greetDelayed: function() {
    const self = this; // preserve reference to `user`
    setTimeout(function() {
      console.log(`Hello, ${self.name}!`);
    }, 1000);
  }
};





✅ Why Arrow Function Fixes It 
setTimeout(() => {
  console.log(`Hello, ${this.name}!`);
}, 1000);
Arrow functions don’t have their own this. Instead, they lexically bind this from the surrounding scope. In this case, the surrounding scope is greetDelayed, and in that context, this === user.




Lexical this Binding:

"Lexical" means based on where a function is written in code, not where it's called. Arrow functions inherit this from their outer function scope, preserving the context of the object that owns the method.

 Example – First Fails, Then Works
 const animal = {
  species: "Cat",
  speakLater: function() {
    setTimeout(function() {
      console.log(`I am a ${this.species}`);
    }, 1000);
  }
};

animal.speakLater(); // I am a undefined


Corrected with Arrow Function:
const animal = {
  species: "Cat",
  speakLater: function() {
    setTimeout(() => {
      console.log(`I am a ${this.species}`);
    }, 1000);
  }
};

animal.speakLater(); // I am a Cat
